To output a PDF in SAS with a journal format, you can use the `ods` statement and specify the following options:

1. `ods pdf`: This tells SAS to output the report to a PDF file.
2. `file=filename.pdf`: Specify the name of the PDF file you want to create.
3. `startpage=no` : This tells SAS to begin the output on the first page.
4. `nocenter` : This option removes the centering of the journal entries, which by default, SAS will center the text.
5. `odate=ddmonyyhhmms` : This option formats the date using the format `dd-mmm-yy hh:mm:ss`

Here is an example code:
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf" startpage=no nocenter odate=ddmonyyhhmms;

proc print data=sashelp.crsdata;
run;

ods pdf close;
```
This will create a PDF file named `journal.pdf` that contains the report generated by the `proc print` statement.

If you want to format the journal entries differently, you can use the `format` statement or the `attrib` statement to customize the appearance of the output. For example:
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf" startpage=no nocenter odate=ddmonyyhhmms;

format journal_entry $50.;

proc print data=sashelp.crsdata;
run;

ods pdf close;
```
This will format the journal entries to have a maximum length of 50 characters.

You can also use the `ods layout` statement to control the layout of the journal entries. For example:
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf" startpage=no nocenter odate=ddmonyyhhmms;
ods layout invisible;

proc print data=sashelp.crsdata;
run;

ods layout end;

ods pdf close;
```
This will remove the borders and padding around the journal entries.

